    <section class="callout dark-callout">
      <div class="eyebrow">Use these as pressure tests</div>

      <h2>A respectful funeral should not wreck the person left paying for it.</h2>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend funeral calculators are educational tools. Use them to compare
        options, understand tradeoffs, and spot debt pressure before signing a
        funeral contract. For legal, estate, insurance, tax, or funeral-director
        questions, talk with a qualified professional.
      </p>
    </section>


      <section id="FuneralDecisionPath" class="content-section">
        <h2>Start with the funeral decision, not one advertised package price.</h2>

        <p>
          Funeral affordability is difficult because many families are making decisions
          under time pressure, grief, family expectations, and incomplete pricing
          information. A quoted package can be useful, but it may not show cemetery
          costs, obituary costs, death certificates, flowers, transportation, permits,
          reception costs, payment-plan terms, or what insurance will actually cover.
        </p>

        <p>
          This hub is designed to route users toward stable funeral-cost calculators
          that separate the main decision paths: burial, cremation, obituary planning,
          payment plans, insurance coverage, and cash-flow pressure. The goal is not to
          judge a memorial choice. The goal is to help a household see the real cost
          before savings are drained or debt is added.
        </p>

        <div class="funeral-hub-grid">
          <div class="funeral-hub-card">
            <h3>Burial cost decision</h3>
            <p>
              Use burial tools when the largest costs may include a casket, cemetery
              plot, opening and closing fees, headstone, service, transportation, and
              funeral-home charges.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="funeral-hub-card">
            <h3>Cremation cost decision</h3>
            <p>
              Use cremation tools when the main question is whether direct cremation,
              memorial service choices, urns, keepsakes, travel, or reception costs
              change the total.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="funeral-hub-card">
            <h3>Payment-plan decision</h3>
            <p>
              Use payment-plan tools when the family is deciding whether monthly
              payments are safer than draining cash or placing funeral expenses on a
              credit card.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="funeral-hub-card">
            <h3>Insurance coverage decision</h3>
            <p>
              Use insurance tools when a policy, benefit, prepaid arrangement, or family
              contribution may cover only part of the final bill.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="FuneralCostBuckets" class="content-section">
        <h2>Separate required funeral costs from flexible memorial choices.</h2>

        <p>
          Funeral costs are easier to compare when required items are separated from
          optional or flexible choices. Families can often protect the meaning of a
          service while changing the venue, timing, obituary length, reception, flowers,
          printed materials, or burial and cremation add-ons.
        </p>

        <ul class="funeral-hub-check-list">
          <li>Separate funeral-home charges from cemetery, crematory, obituary, and reception costs.</li>
          <li>Ask which fees are required, optional, third-party, refundable, or due immediately.</li>
          <li>Compare burial and cremation totals using the same service assumptions.</li>
          <li>Count death certificates, permits, transportation, flowers, programs, and obituary placement.</li>
          <li>Confirm whether insurance, benefits, prepaid plans, or family contributions are available and when they pay.</li>
          <li>Check whether the plan leaves enough emergency savings for surviving household needs.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="FuneralRiskDrivers" class="content-section">
        <h2>The safest funeral budget protects the surviving household.</h2>

        <p>
          A funeral can be meaningful and still financially dangerous if it drains the
          household that remains. The financial risk is higher when the family uses
          high-interest debt, assumes insurance will pay immediately, or commits before
          comparing what is required with what is optional.
        </p>

        <div class="funeral-hub-grid">
          <div class="funeral-hub-card">
            <h3>Cash-timing risk</h3>
            <p>
              Insurance, estate funds, family contributions, or benefits may not arrive
              before bills are due. Timing matters as much as the total coverage amount.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="funeral-hub-card">
            <h3>Debt risk</h3>
            <p>
              Funeral costs become more stressful when they move to a credit card,
              personal loan, or payment plan that competes with rent, groceries, utilities,
              and other household bills.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="funeral-hub-card">
            <h3>Package risk</h3>
            <p>
              A package may omit cemetery fees, obituary charges, flowers, permits,
              reception costs, transportation, keepsakes, or upgraded service options.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="funeral-hub-card">
            <h3>Family-pressure risk</h3>
            <p>
              Grief and family expectations can make it harder to say no. A written
              budget gives families a calmer way to compare choices before committing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="FuneralScenarioPlanning" class="content-section">
        <h2>Run funeral plans through three scenarios.</h2>

        <p>
          A single estimate can hide the real range of funeral costs. Before signing a
          contract or choosing a payment plan, compare a manageable scenario, a realistic
          scenario, and a stress scenario.
        </p>

        <div class="funeral-hub-grid">
          <div class="funeral-hub-card">
            <h3>Manageable scenario</h3>
            <p>
              The bill is confirmed, insurance or contributions are available on time,
              no high-interest debt is needed, and the surviving household keeps enough
              cash for basic needs.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="funeral-hub-card">
            <h3>Realistic scenario</h3>
            <p>
              Some third-party costs run higher, an obituary or reception adds more than
              expected, and the family must trim flexible choices to avoid debt.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="funeral-hub-card">
            <h3>Stress scenario</h3>
            <p>
              Insurance is delayed, family help changes, cemetery or transportation
              costs are higher, or the payment plan creates monthly pressure afterward.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

